        <h3>Soft Rock Carries the Ballads</h3>
        <p>{{TOP_GENRE_N}} of the {{N}} songs are tagged {{TOP_GENRE}}.</p>
        <p>Six of them reached number one.</p>
        <p>Read <a href="/blog/genres/70s-soft-rock/">70s soft rock</a> for the sound behind them.</p>
        <p>Jim Croce's "Time in a Bottle" is one of them, and the <a href="/blog/artists/jim-croce/">Jim Croce</a> page tells his story.</p>

        <h3>Soul and Pop Fill the Rest</h3>
        <p>Eight songs are tagged soul.</p>
        <p>They include Bill Withers's "Ain't No Sunshine" at number 23 for 1971 and Billy Paul's "Me and Mrs. Jones."</p>
        <p>Billy Paul reached number one on December 16, 1972, for three weeks.</p>
        <p>The <a href="/blog/genres/70s-soul-music/">70s soul music</a> page goes wider.</p>
        <p>Pop holds "Alone Again (Naturally)," "The Way We Were" and "Torn Between Two Lovers."</p>

        <h2 id="eras">Four Stretches of the Decade</h2>
        <p>Grouping the {{N}} songs by the year-end Hot 100 they appear on shows when the slow songs reached the top.</p>

