        <h3>1970 to 1972: Slow Songs Ruled the Top</h3>
        <p>Five of the eleven number ones came in these three years.</p>
        <p>"Bridge Over Troubled Water" opened the decade with six weeks from February 28, 1970.</p>
        <p>The Simon & Garfunkel story is on the <a href="/blog/songs/bridge-over-troubled-water/">Bridge Over Troubled Water</a> page.</p>
        <p>In 1972 "Without You" held four weeks from February 19.</p>
        <p>"Heart of Gold" took over for one week on March 18, so two slow songs ran back to back.</p>

        <h3>1973 to 1975: Croce, Streisand and a Trio of Tears</h3>
        <p>"Time in a Bottle" reached number one on December 29, 1973, for two weeks.</p>
        <p>It followed "The Most Beautiful Girl" and gave way to "The Joker."</p>
        <p>"The Way We Were" is the third number one, first reaching the top on February 2, 1974.</p>
        <p>Not sure what to play after it? Spin the <a href="/pages/random-70s-song">Random 70s Song Generator</a>.</p>

        <h3>1976 and 1977: A Quiet Middle</h3>
        <p>Six songs sit in these two years, and two reached number one.</p>
        <p>Diana Ross's "Theme from Mahogany" took the top spot on January 24, 1976.</p>
        <p>Mary MacGregor's "Torn Between Two Lovers" followed on February 5, 1977, for two weeks.</p>

        <h3>1978 and 1979: Neighbours on the Year-End List</h3>
        <p>Only one of the six songs here reached number one, Robert John's "Sad Eyes" on October 6, 1979.</p>
        <p>Roberta Flack and Donny Hathaway's "The Closer I Get to You" is number 38 for 1978.</p>
        <p>Kansas's "Dust in the Wind" is number 39 on the same year-end list, right beside it.</p>

