        <h3>Six Weeks Each for Simon & Garfunkel and Gilbert O'Sullivan</h3>
        <p>"Bridge Over Troubled Water" held six straight weeks from February 28, 1970.</p>
        <p>Gilbert O'Sullivan's "Alone Again (Naturally)" spent six weeks at number one in all, first reaching the top on July 29, 1972.</p>

        <h3>One Week and Gone</h3>
        <p>Four of the eleven held the top spot for a single week.</p>
        <p>They are "You've Got a Friend," "Heart of Gold," "Theme from Mahogany" and "Sad Eyes."</p>

        <h2 id="missed">Slow Songs That Never Hit Number One</h2>
        <p>{{NOT_N1}} songs on the list never topped the weekly Hot 100.</p>
        <p>Each is here because the year-end list proves it was played everywhere.</p>
        <ul>
{{NOT_N1_LIST}}
        </ul>

        <h3>A Rank Is Not a Peak</h3>
        <p>The year-end list measures a whole year of chart life, not the highest weekly position.</p>
        <p>So these ranks say how big the song was, not whether it climbed to the top.</p>
        <p>For the decade's biggest hits, see the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-number-one-hits/">70s number one hits</a> page.</p>
        <p>When the lights come up, the <a href="/blog/songs/70s-dance-songs/">70s dance songs</a> and <a href="/blog/songs/70s-road-trip-songs/">70s road trip songs</a> pick up.</p>
        <p>More of the decade's songs live on the <a href="/">Music of the 70s</a> home page.</p>

